if (num_vec <= 0)
```
Reason:
The variable `num_vec` is declared as an unsigned integer (`u32`). If `pci_msix_vec_count(cptvf->pdev)` returns a negative value (indicating an error), it will be implicitly converted to a large positive value when assigned to `num_vec`. Consequently, the comparison `num_vec <= 0` will always evaluate to false, leading to incorrect error handling.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
int num_vec;
num_vec = pci_msix_vec_count(cptvf->pdev);
if (num_vec <= 0)
```
Reason:
Changing the type of `num_vec` from `u32` to `int` allows it to correctly represent negative values. This ensures that if `pci_msix_vec_count(cptvf->pdev)` returns a negative value, the condition `num_vec <= 0` will evaluate to true, enabling proper error handling for the function.

